<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=lathe">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Tools and materials</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lathe</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">6th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A lathe is a machine tool that turns a workpiece about an axis while tools cut, sand, drill or shape it into a form symmetrical about that axis. Clear evidence of turning comes from the 6th century BCE: fragments of a wooden bowl in an Etruscan tomb in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and two wooden dishes with turned rims from present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>. A painting in the tomb of Petosiris in 4th-century BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> is the first known picture of a lathe, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> rotary lathes sharpened tools and weapons by about 400 BCE. Andrey Nartov built a lathe with a tool-carrying carriage and gears in 1718, and by the 1770s precision lathes with a slide-rest had become practical.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lathe.jpg" alt="Lathe" width="330" height="255" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Glenn McKechnie · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:HwacheonCentreLathe_460x1000.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>6th century BCE</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Etruscan tomb, northern Italy</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd><a href="/culture/etruscans">Etruscans</a></dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Also arose independently</span>China (c. 400 BCE)</p><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>Tenuous evidence of a turned artifact at a Mycenaean Greek site may go back to the 13th or 14th century BCE.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/potters-wheel">Potter's wheel<span>c. 4500 BCE</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=lathe">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
